The code statements that lead to the bug are:
qdisc_put(cl->qdisc);
kfree(cl);
Reason:
In the `destroy_class` error path of `qfq_change_class()`, these two lines are executed unconditionally. When `cl` refers to an already existing class (`existing == true`), freeing `cl` and its `qdisc` while the class is still part of the scheduler's data structures leads to a use-after-free vulnerability.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!existing) {
    qdisc_put(cl->qdisc);
    kfree(cl);
}
Reason:
The conditional check `if (!existing)` ensures that only newly allocated classes (where `cl` was allocated within the current function call) are freed. If the class already existed (`existing == true`), it is not freed, thus preventing the use-after-free. The existing class remains valid and is properly handled by the earlier modification logic.

